; Limit 04: no type parameters, so every array helper is written once per
; element type.
;
; Growing an array (copy into one twice the size) is the same code for every
; element type, but a def names its types, and a data type cannot take a
; parameter either. Across these three programs that meant:
;
;   - grow and copy for (array int), and again for (array Entry) in limit 02
;   - count_fields in dijkstra.l0, a copy of the prelude's length for
;     (list (list int)); the prelude itself has rev_strs_onto, a copy of
;     rev_onto for the same reason
;   - the name table in dijkstra.l0 is a hash map from (list int) to int only;
;     a second map with other types would be a second copy of Names, slot_of,
;     name_add, roomy, rehash, and put (about 50 lines)
;   - the sort in mst.l0 sorts indices by an int key array, which is generic
;     only because indirection makes it so; sorting records directly, or by a
;     compound key, would need a copy per element type and per order, since
;     there are no function values to pass a comparison either
;
; This program is the smallest instance: grow_ints works, and using it on an
; (array (list int)) is a type error, so the second copy has to be written.
;
;   $ tools/l0 check 04-no-type-parameters.l0
;   04-no-type-parameters.l0:34:14: error: argument 1 of grow_ints must be
;   (array int), but this is (array (list int))

(def grow_ints ((a (array int)) (len int)) (array int)
  (copy_ints a (array_new len 0) 0))

(def copy_ints ((src (array int)) (dst (array int)) (i int)) (array int)
  (if (ge i (array_len src)) dst (copy_ints src (array_set dst i (array_get src i)) (add i 1))))

(def main () int
  (let names (grow_ints (array_new 4 (nil int)) 8)
    (array_len names)))
